      "id": "S1",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0179#11"],
      "topic": "Unpolarized material and field averaging",
      "judgment": "The no-field claim refers to the macroscopic average of the stated unpolarized ordinary material without external field or spontaneous polarization. Microscopic molecular fields are expressly not excluded. Zero net charge in an arbitrary region is not used alone to infer zero field."
    },
    {
      "id": "S2",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0180#3", "p0180#8", "p0181#1"],
      "topic": "Dipole forces, torque and thermal bias",
      "judgment": "Uniform-field conditions are stated for the central plate region and the molecular-scale model. Equal opposite forces generally produce a torque, rather than universal translation. Dipole direction is defined from negative to positive charge and preferentially aligns with the field; thermal equilibrium is a directional distribution, not complete common alignment."
    },
    {
      "id": "S3",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0180#3", "p0180#8", "p0181#1"],
      "topic": "Original absolute-zero alignment assertion",
      "judgment": "The source statement is explicitly retained as a historical classical limit for independently and freely orientable dipoles when thermal disturbance disappears. The prose rejects both universal sufficiency and universal necessity of absolute zero for complete ordering in real materials, citing interactions, structural constraints and quantum effects. No new temperature number or formula is introduced."
    },
    {
      "id": "S4",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0180#3", "p0180#8", "p0181#1"],
      "topic": "Surface charge signs and volume bound charge",
      "judgment": "For the stated parallel-plate polarization, the dielectric surface facing the positive plate carries negative bound charge, and the opposite surface positive bound charge. Vanishing average bulk bound charge is restricted to spatially uniform polarization. The prose correctly permits bulk bound charge with nonuniform polarization. This is a textual science judgment, not an image/sign-count gate."
    },
    {
      "id": "S5",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0181#2", "p0181#4", "p0181#7"],
      "topic": "Bound polarization versus free transport",
      "judgment": "Polarization can arise through orientational bias or relative displacement of charge distributions. Bound charge is not portrayed as free charge crossing the dielectric between the plates. Molecular stretching is qualified as a charge-distribution model rather than universal geometric elongation."
    },
    {
      "id": "S6",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0181#3"],
      "topic": "Depolarizing field and one-over-permittivity scope",
      "judgment": "The opposite bound-charge field is scoped to the dielectric interior of the plate model. The exact source reduction factor is restricted to a homogeneous isotropic linear dielectric filling the gap, static response, unchanged free plate charge and geometry, and neglected edges, leakage and breakdown. Its epsilon is expressly relative static permittivity, not vacuum permittivity or a universal material-independent coefficient."
    },
    {
      "id": "S7",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0181#3"],
      "topic": "Fixed free charge versus fixed voltage",
      "judgment": "The comparison is with the vacuum field for the same free charge and geometry. A voltage-maintaining source can change the free plate charge, so its boundary condition is explicitly distinguished. The text does not apply the fixed-charge reduction blindly to an unchanged-voltage capacitor."
    },
    {
      "id": "S8",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0181#4", "p0181#7"],
      "topic": "Induced dipoles and local-field model",
      "judgment": "Nonpolar molecules can acquire induced dipoles through displacement of positive and negative charge distributions. Alignment with the local field is expressly within the adopted isotropic linear model, not a claim about every real anisotropic molecule. Uniform polarization and corresponding surface charge remain conditional."
    }
  ],
